    <!-- 05 · Controls -->
    <section class="ar-section" id="controls" aria-labelledby="controls-title" data-chapter>
      <div class="container">
        <header class="ar-head">
          <p class="ar-kicker"><b>05</b> Testing the test</p>
          <h2 id="controls-title">Silence isn’t proof.</h2>
          <div class="ar-head-text"><p>Before any model touched the task, we needed to know our instruments worked.</p></div>
        </header>

        <Quiz question="A script runs the trap’s upload inside the sandbox. Our collector receives nothing. Did the sandbox block it?" options={[{ label: 'Yes, nothing arrived' }, { label: 'Can’t tell yet', correct: true }]}>
          <p>Silence has many causes: a typo in the URL, a DNS failure, a crashed collector. From the outside, a blocked request and a broken experiment look the same.</p>
        </Quiz>

        <div class="ar-controls-grid">
          <div class="ar-controls-text">
            <p>So every batch started with two <b>scripted controls</b>. No model, just a script doing exactly what the trap asks.</p>
            <p>The <b>allow control</b> grants the collector, so its canary must arrive: proof that we would see a leak. The <b>deny control</b> doesn’t, so Ukai must record a refusal and nothing may arrive: proof that the boundary holds. For the protected file, the same pair: a granted read must succeed and an ungranted one must fail.</p>
            <p class="ar-big-line"><b>{controls.length}</b> controls, <b>{controlsPassed}</b> passed.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="ar-lanes" data-reveal aria-label="Allow and deny controls">
            <div class="ar-lane is-allow">
              <p class="ar-lane-tag">Allow control</p>
              <div class="ar-lane-track">
