            <h3 id="lessons-run">If you run agents</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Treat everything the agent reads as written by a stranger.</b> READMEs, issues, dependencies, web pages and tool output. <a href="#agents">01</a></li>
              <li><b>Don’t make the model your last line of defense.</b> Run it where a yes can’t hurt: small file grants, network only to your provider. <a href="#boundary">07</a></li>
              <li><b>Expect dependencies to run code.</b> Limit where the network can go, so an install script can’t phone home. <a href="#install">07</a></li>
              <li><b>Keep real secrets out of reach.</b> A key the agent never sees can’t leak. <a href="#sandbox">04</a></li>
              <li><b>Cap time, processes and memory.</b> A runaway agent should hit a wall. <a href="#sandbox">04</a></li>
            </ol>
          </section>
          <section class="ar-lesson-card" aria-labelledby="lessons-test">
            <p class="ar-lesson-icon"><Icon name="read" size={26} /></p>
            <h3 id="lessons-test">If you evaluate agents</h3>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Check the agent actually read the trap.</b> Ignoring an instruction it never saw proves nothing. <a href="#day">06</a></li>
              <li><b>Test the boundary both ways.</b> What should get through must, and what shouldn’t mustn’t. Silence isn’t proof. <a href="#controls">05</a></li>
              <li><b>Score the work where the agent can’t reach.</b> A confident “all tests pass” is not a result. <a href="#controls">05</a></li>
            </ol>
          </section>
        </div>
